tunefft: fix inverted success assertion that aborts on every run

gf2x_mul_fft() returns 0 on success and a negative error code on
failure (see gf2x/gf2x-impl.h).  The two timing call sites in
src/tunefft.c wrap the return value as

    wantzero(gf2x_mul_fft(...) == 0);

but wantzero(x) aborts when x != 0.  On success, (rc == 0) evaluates
to 1, so the tuner calls abort() on the very first *successful* FFT
multiplication -- deterministically, on every platform, immediately
after the first Toom-Cook measurement:

    Tuning FFT multiplication to wordsize 8000000
    501..501 TC:2.6e-05 Aborted (core dumped)

(501 = GF2X_MUL_FFT_BEGIN_TUNE/2 + 1, the first tuned size.  abort()
prints nothing, and only the "TC:" fragment survives because tunefft
fflushes stdout right before the FFT calls.)

Fix: pass the return code to wantzero() directly, so it aborts only
on a genuine error (negative return), which is the intended
assertion.  All tuned sizes are >= 501 words per operand, far above
GF2X_TERNARY_FFT_MINIMUM_SIZE (28), so the "arguments too small"
error path cannot fire spuriously.

Verified after the fix on gf2x 1.3.99 (Ubuntu 24.04, gcc 13.3):
  - ./tunefft -s 1.4 700 -o tunefft.res completes, exit 0;
  - both call sites are exercised (F2/split timings run for K >= 64)
    and tunefft's internal check() validates every FFT product
    against the Toom reference and F1 against F2, all passing;
  - modify-thresholds consumes the .res and rewrites
    GF2X_MUL_FFT_TABLE / GF2X_FFT_TUNING_INFO as intended.

Note: .gitlab-ci.yml contains no tune targets, so nothing in CI
exercises tunefft, which is presumably how this survived.

Apply from the gf2x source root with:  patch -p1 < this-file
---
--- a/src/tunefft.c
+++ b/src/tunefft.c
